WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.068
-One of our first guests tonight

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.402
is an Academy Award-nominated
actor.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:05.037
The other is
an acclaimed musician.

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:06.439
Starting in February,
they'll be on tour

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.641
performing REM's iconic
album "Murmur."

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.009
Please welcome to the show

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.845
Michael Shannon
and Jason Narducy.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:15.514
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.618
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:32.131
♪♪

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:34.066
Welcome to the show.
-Thank you.

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:36.302
-So this is -- You're
touring classic albums.

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:37.537
You've done this before.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:38.938
This is not the first time
you've done it.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.907
So I want to talk
about the friendship

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.775
and then the idea for the album.
But let's start

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.677
with the friendship.
How did you guys first connect?

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.413
-We met through a fellow
named Robbie Fulks,

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.249
who's an incredible
singer-songwriter.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.317
He was based in Chicago
at the time.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.918
I think he's
moved to L.A. since.

00:00:52.918 --> 00:00:57.156
But, yeah, we played --
He had a show, a residency,

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.958
where he would play
a different record all the time.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.394
And he wanted to play
this Lou Reed record.

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.464
And he asked me to sing it,
and Jason to play bass on it,

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:05.898
and that's how we met, yeah.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.600
-And so you guys,
you basically met

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.068
doing some version
of what you're doing now.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:10.336
-Exactly.
-And when did you do

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.937
your first one where it was
just the two of you?

00:01:11.937 --> 00:01:15.308
-It was me asking you to do
"Queen is Dead," right?

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.243
-Yes, yeah.
-Yeah, we did

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.312
"The Queen is Dead"
by the Smiths,

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.715
and we did that up in Evanston,
where we all --

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.416
-We used to all live
in Evanston, Illinois.

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:26.052
Early '90s.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.453
-There we go.
Yeah, make some noise, Evanston.

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:30.289
-Yeah.
-You did this one.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:32.124
You did "Murmur in Chicago
this past summer.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:34.160
-Yep.
-How long of a show was it?

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.563
-Very long. [ Laughs ]
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:38.731
-Yeah, we knew, going into it,
because "Murmur"

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.800
is a pretty short album.
It's under an hour.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.970
And so we also learned
a bunch of other songs,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.140
and we wound up playing
for like three hours.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.876
I mean, there were lots
of little speeches.

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.810
-What kind of little speeches?

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.846
-Well, the owner of the club,
Joe Shanahan --

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:55.314
we played at Metro.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:58.984
And it was also -- it was the
40th anniversary of "Murmur,"

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.587
it was the 40th anniversary
of Metro,

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:04.123
and the first show at Metro
was R.E.M.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.091
And it was probably
when they were on tour for this.

00:02:06.091 --> 00:02:08.094
-Yeah, yeah.
-So Joe came out

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:11.431
and made some speeches.
He was reminiscing and stuff.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:13.332
And then --
-He came out after every song.

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:15.135
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.403
-"And another thing..."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.605
-Does R.E.M. have any awareness
that you're doing this?

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:23.642
-Well, that night, Mr. --
Can we say?

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:25.611
-Yeah.
-Mr. Mills was there.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:26.912
-Wow!
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:29.215
-Did he appreciate the effort?
-He kept coming up.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:31.184
-Oh, wow.
-Run up and sing with us.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.719
-Oh, wait, so you actually
had -- That's amazing.

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:35.788
-It's amazing.
-How early did --

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.256
How late, I should say,
did you know

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:38.724
he was gonna be there and
maybe singing with you guys?

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:41.193
-He walked into the greenroom
right before we went onstage,

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:45.264
and everybody just
kind of was in shock.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:48.801
And then Jason's like,
"Do you want to do anything?"

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.336
And he's like, "No, I know
you guys have been working

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.171
really hard on this.
I'm just here to enjoy it."

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.608
And then, about five songs
into the show,

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.609
the crowd started cheering
and I'm like,

00:02:57.609 --> 00:02:59.445
"Oh, man,
I'm really killing it."

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.415
[ Laughter ]
And then I really -- Took me

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.517
like a few seconds,
and I turned to my right,

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.285
and Mike Mills was standing
there, singing,

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:08.521
and I thought, "Oh,
this has nothing to do with me."

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.822
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.657
"There's an original member
of the band onstage."

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:13.425
-Pretty great.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:13.425 --> 00:03:14.661
-It's so nice to know --
when he showed up

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:16.095
in the greenroom,
did any part of you think

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.364
he was there to be like,
"This will not stand!"?

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.732
-Yeah.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.867
Or picture him in the
front row just going...

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:22.969
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.271
-I will say, it brings me
great joy to know

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:29.041
that his plan was to not,
and that the power of the music,

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:31.911
the opportunity to do it again.
-A couple drinks.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.512
-Yeah, a couple drinks.
Sure, that never hurts.

00:03:33.512 --> 00:03:35.080
But that must have been --
I don't know,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.615
it's cool that you guys
made something so magnetic

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:37.817
that he came up
onstage with you.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.485
-I mean, it was an epic night.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:42.722
I hope we can re-create it
on the tour.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.890
I don't think we're going to
play three hours every night,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.792
because I'll --
[Hoarsely] I'll be like this.

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:49.128
-Yeah, because
you give your all.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.930
-[ Normal voice ] Yeah,
by the last song of the encore,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.533
I was -- thank God,
like five other people

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.034
had come up onstage
to sing with us,

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.401
because I don't think --

00:03:56.401 --> 00:03:58.438
I was pretty close
to life support.

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:01.907
-Yeah, you were giving it all.
-Yeah.

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:04.276
-Obviously, you're the
professional musician here,

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:07.079
and you started at an age
that is truly incredible.

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:08.981
And not just fiddling around.
You were in a band.

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:10.549
How old were you
in your first band?

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:12.919
-I started a band
when I was 10, called Verboten.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.320
-Verboten?
-Yeah.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.722
-By the way, that so speaks
to the era that we grew up.

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:18.691
-Umlauts.
-Umlaut.

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.492
If you've got an umlaut
in your band, you rock.

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:21.828
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:23.228
-And this is -- I will say,

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:25.465
when I heard you were 10
in a band called Verboten,

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:27.033
I didn't think
you'd look this cool,

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.401
because this is, like -- I'd
go see these guys in concert.

00:04:29.401 --> 00:04:30.903
-Wow!
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.705
-That's you right there, yeah?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:39.178
-This is this is Zach,
this is Chris, this is Tracey.

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.247
-Uh-huh.
-These two lived

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:42.915
on the same block as me.
-That's great.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.184
-That's Zach's dad's Volvo
behind us.

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:46.885
[ Laughter ]
-Safety first.

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:49.288
When you got a band that young,
you can't do a van.

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:50.656
You got to do a Volvo.
-Yeah.

00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:52.725
-But you played,
like, actual venues.

00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:56.462
-Yeah. I'm just so lucky
that I met those guys.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.997
You know, like,
it was such a --

00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:00.166
It was a challenging time,
I think, in all of our lives.

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:02.601
And we were kind of, like,
holding each other up

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:06.338
and trying to create
punk rock in 1983.

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:08.708
And Tracey was so charismatic

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:11.077
that she would win over
these other bands.

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:14.380
And so, yeah, we played at the
Cubby Bear in Chicago in 1983,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.715
opening up for a great band
called Naked Raygun.

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:20.152
-And --
-And were your parents

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.488
supportive of the idea?
Would they come to gigs?

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.256
-I think
they were confused by it.

00:05:24.256 --> 00:05:26.292
But, you know,
because I was 11...

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:28.460
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.562
...my dad had to drive me
to the show.

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:34.467
-Yeah, yeah.
-And I'm very thankful

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:37.536
that he brought
that 1983 camcorder

00:05:37.536 --> 00:05:40.373
and put it on his shoulder
and videotaped us.

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:42.541
-You did bring a camcorder.
We do have video.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:45.211
And I want to say, on the video,
you guys continue to rock

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.679
at a level I did not expect.
Let's take a quick look.

00:05:47.679 --> 00:05:50.750
-[ Singing indistinctly ]

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.286
♪♪

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.889
♪ Ooh ♪

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.857
♪♪

00:05:58.857 --> 00:06:01.861
[ Singing indistinctly ]

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.629
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.832
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:07.600
-Yeah.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.569
-Urban legend or true story?

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:13.072
A young Dave Grohl
saw this band?

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:15.741
-Yeah, Tracey's family and
Dave's family are really --

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:18.378
They call each other cousins.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:20.846
-And so, when
Dave was 13 years old,

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:23.048
he came with his family
and saw us practice.

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:28.053
And he's very sweet to cite us
as an inspiration to him,

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:30.022
and has even --
he wrote a chapter

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:32.190
in his book "Storyteller"
about Verboten.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.893
And, yeah, it's crazy.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.361
We're all still
really good friends,

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.863
which means a lot to me.
But we'll text each other

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:39.898
'cause some book will come out
and it's like, "Check this out.

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:41.567
This band that we had
40 years ago

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.136
is -- thanks to Dave Grohl,
is being talked about still."

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.538
-That's really amazing.
-Yeah.

00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:47.873
-You have -- Yeah,
give it up for Verboten.

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:50.543
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:53.345
We've talked in the past
about your first band

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:55.715
was Jehovah's Suspects.
-Yes.

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:57.583
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:00.720
-Now, I believe you --
You sang "Armadillo Song" here.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.356
That was one of your hits.
And maybe your only song?

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:05.024
-It was the only song I wrote
then.

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:07.259
I was like 15
when I wrote that song.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:09.629
-Ancient.
-Yeah.

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:11.930
-For these 11-year-old bands,
a 15-year-old...

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.232
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:15.934
And I don't know,
we knew three other songs,

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:18.538
but we didn't have --
I was in Kentucky.

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.506
We didn't have the scene
like they had in Chicago.

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:23.643
You know, we basically would go
to, like, somebody's barn or --

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:27.413
You know, there were no clubs
that we could go to or anything.

00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:32.284
But, yeah, I was more --
I very quickly

00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:35.220
leaned towards theater,
because it just seemed like,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.724
frankly, a lot less work.
-Yeah.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.827
In which way?
Like, when you do a school play,

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:43.128
there's more infrastructure?
-You got to do your audition.

00:07:43.128 --> 00:07:45.297
And if they give you the part,
you just do your part.

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:47.499
But if you're trying to get
a band going, you know,

00:07:47.499 --> 00:07:50.636
it's a lot of work,
a lot of determination.

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:54.774
I think, even more rejection
than you deal with, with acting.

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:57.510
And nobody's gonna --
There's no director

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.278
or producer
to take care of you.

00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:01.647
You got to figure it all out
yourself, you know?

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:05.551
-Is it true that your dad
made Verboten break up

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:07.453
because he wanted you
to finish sixth grade?

00:08:07.453 --> 00:08:09.021
[ Laughter ]

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:10.655
-Yeah, we did have
a band meeting

00:08:10.655 --> 00:08:12.692
with all the parents.

00:08:12.692 --> 00:08:14.994
And Tracey -- Tracey was
a little bit older than us.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.429
She was 15, 16, and
she wanted to get a van

00:08:17.429 --> 00:08:21.467
and -- and take off,
hit the road.

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.569
And my dad was like, "He's gonna
finish sixth grade."

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:25.004
[ Laughter ]

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:27.105
-Usually, in these stories,
you take the side of the band.

00:08:27.105 --> 00:08:28.574
You're like, "Hey, old man,
you don't get it!"

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:30.309
But now that I have kids,
I'm like, "Yeah,

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:31.943
you got to finish sixth."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:31.943 --> 00:08:34.479
-Well, the funny thing now
is that my dad works

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:36.015
in a bookstore,

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:38.684
and when a customer comes up
with a book that we're in,

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:43.589
he's just like,
"Please turn to page 209."

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:45.390
[ Laughter ]

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:48.194
And you know, you can imagine
being a book shopper

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:50.328
and you're kind of flipping
through like, "Why?

00:08:50.328 --> 00:08:51.830
Why am I doing this?"

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.499
And then he just puts his finger
on my name in the book,

00:08:54.499 --> 00:08:57.336
and they go,
"I don't know who that is."

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:59.237
[ Laughter ]

00:08:59.237 --> 00:09:00.706
-It must be really special
for him.

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:02.708
[ Laughter ]

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:04.443
-Yeah, they go,
"I don't know who this is,"

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:06.579
Just like the audience did
when I walked out here.

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.380
[ Laughter ]

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.382
-It'll be cool
for him to watch this tonight

00:09:10.382 --> 00:09:11.884
and be like, "Look at that.
No recognition."

00:09:11.884 --> 00:09:14.553
-Yeah, well,
it's impenetrable pride.

00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:16.155
-Yeah. There you go.
I love it.

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:17.856
A dad proud of his kid's
rock 'n' roll band

00:09:17.856 --> 00:09:19.558
all these years later
is a pretty cool thing.

00:09:19.558 --> 00:09:22.495
You have facial hair right now
going on

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:23.996
that you want to explain
real quick.

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:25.831
-And fingernails.
-Oh, yeah, the fingernails.

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:28.634
-If you see them tonight during
the show, don't be alarmed.

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:30.435
They're for a play I'm doing
right now,

00:09:30.435 --> 00:09:32.838
called "Waiting for Godot."
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:36.342
Oh, thanks. Hey.
Right on.

00:09:36.342 --> 00:09:38.510
Yeah, Jason came to see it
last night.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.981
Most of the band did, actually.
It was awfully nice of you.

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:45.050
And we're going
till December 23rd.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:48.087
Or the precipice of Christmas,
as I like to refer to it.

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:49.888
-Yeah.
You've always called it that.

00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:52.891
-Yes, December 23rd is --
-A lot of people only start

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:54.259
talking -- they're
like "Christmas Eve,"

00:09:54.259 --> 00:09:57.162
and you're like, no, you got to
go one day before that."

00:09:57.162 --> 00:10:00.298
I like the precipice.
-Yeah. Add that doom feeling.

00:10:00.298 --> 00:10:01.934
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:03.936
"Eve" is too hopeful,
whereas, like, "precipice,"

00:10:03.936 --> 00:10:05.771
I feel like is a truer bounce
for what you're about to happen.

00:10:05.771 --> 00:10:07.940
-We're falling, yes, yes.
-I am glad

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.575
because I do also feel --
and don't take this

00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:11.110
the wrong way, Michael --
I feel like if you came out

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:12.811
with those fingernails
and that beard,

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:14.313
a lot of people
wouldn't think it was a play.

00:10:14.313 --> 00:10:17.483
[ Laughter ]
-That's true. That's true.

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.652
It's my COVID look.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:19.652 --> 00:10:21.820
Everyone's like,
"Oh, he doesn't know

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.923
we're on the other side of it."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:24.923 --> 00:10:27.359
-And you guys are going to do
a song for us

00:10:27.359 --> 00:10:29.828
from your performance,
from your show.

00:10:29.828 --> 00:10:31.330
I can't wait to see it.
You guys,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.565
that is Michael Shannon,
that is Jason Narducy.

00:10:33.565 --> 00:10:35.667
For tickets to their tour --
and it's going to be amazing --

00:10:35.667 --> 00:10:38.537
visit concertedefforts.com.
They'll be performing

00:10:38.537 --> 00:10:39.938
later in the show.
Stick around.

00:10:39.938 --> 00:10:41.607
We'll be right back
with Rachel Bloom, everybody.

